  :adr/title "murakumo-main falls back once to OpenRouter GLM 5.3 Flash"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-08-31"
  :adr/related "adr-2608272400-a-provider-that-names-its-own-outage adr-2608291300-bots-return-to-murakumo-main-and-the-pool-stops-rotating"
  :adr/body "## Owner instruction\n\n2026-08-31: 「安定経路, openrouter の glm 5.3 flash を failbackに」\n\n## Decision\n\n`murakumo-main` remains the primary model for Cloud Itonami Bots. A retryable failure before any streamed output falls through exactly once to `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash`. Both selectors use the existing `https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1` OpenAI-compatible provider boundary: Cloud Itonami does not receive or use an OpenRouter credential directly.\n\nThe provider result keeps `requested-model = murakumo-main`, reports the model that actually served as `model = z-ai/glm-5.3-flash`, and marks `fallback? = true` with the primary error type. A GLM failure is reported as `provider/fallback-failed`; it does not recurse into another fallback during the same turn.\n\nThe resident operator configuration is aligned with the same direction: routing, provider default, and workforce model return to `murakumo-main`; the fallback map names GLM. This reverses the temporary 2026-08-31 operator setting that made GLM primary.\n\n## Qualification boundary\n\nA deterministic HTTP fault-injection test proves the exact request order and attribution. An authenticated model-specific generation probe must pass through api.murakumo.cloud before the resident release is switched. These two facts prove the fallback mechanism and fallback endpoint separately; a live Bot turn counts as an observed fallback only when its primary actually fails and its ledger records GLM as the served model."}]
